# Hypothetical learning examples. No population-wide stereotypes are implied.
EXAMPLES={}
def examples(text):
 for line in text.strip().splitlines():
  term,scene,why=[x.strip() for x in line.split('|')];EXAMPLES[term]=(scene,why)
examples('''
Language | A Deaf student in Lahore discusses a research idea using Pakistan Sign Language, while another student explains it through spoken Urdu. | Both use organized form-meaning relationships; sound is a modality difference rather than the defining criterion for language.
Monolingualism | In a hypothetical interview, a Lahore resident reports using Urdu only but recognizes several Urdu accents. | Several accents within one language do not by themselves establish a repertoire of two languages.
Bilingualism | Hina discusses family routines in Punjabi and writes university assignments in English, with different strengths in each. | Functional use of two languages does not require identical skill in every task.
Multilingualism | A Karachi student uses Urdu, Sindhi and English in different parts of daily life. | Three languages fit both the broad multilingual convention and the narrower three-or-more classroom convention.
Trilingualism | A child has meaningful opportunities to communicate in Punjabi, Urdu and English with different people. | The profile involves three languages; opportunities do not guarantee equal proficiency or literacy.
Simultaneous bilingual acquisition | From infancy, a child has regular conversations and play in both Punjabi and Urdu with caregivers. | Early overlapping exposure defines the acquisition history; current dominance must be assessed separately.
Sequential bilingual acquisition | A Punjabi-speaking child starts regular Urdu interaction at preschool after Punjabi development has begun. | The additional language follows an earlier developmental start rather than beginning concurrently from infancy.
Early bilingualism | A four-year-old begins sustained English interaction at preschool while continuing family Urdu. | This describes a childhood start, not a guarantee of balance or future maintenance.
Late bilingualism | An adult in Lahore begins sustained Arabic study and gradually uses it in meaningful conversation. | Later learning can produce bilingual abilities; age alone does not determine success across all skills.
Receptive bilingualism | A child understands a grandmother's Punjabi instructions and answers them appropriately in Urdu. | Understanding is demonstrated while comparable Punjabi production has not been demonstrated.
Dominant bilingualism | A medical student retrieves academic terms more easily in English but family expressions more easily in Urdu. | Dominance is task-sensitive; one overall label may hide important differences.
Balanced bilingualism | A student performs similarly on two matched familiar conversation tasks in Urdu and Punjabi. | Balance is relative to what was measured and cannot be generalized automatically to literacy or every topic.
Additive bilingualism | A school adds English learning while a family continues meaningful Punjabi conversation and access to Punjabi materials. | The earlier language retains support while another is added; identical scores are not required.
Subtractive bilingualism | A hypothetical child is discouraged from Punjabi, increasingly uses only English and loses access to familiar Punjabi expressions. | The case identifies reduced support and decline, not an inevitable effect of learning English.
Heritage language | A British Pakistani teenager values family Punjabi and uses it with relatives while reading English more confidently. | Family affiliation and literacy dominance describe different aspects of the repertoire.
Majority language | In a hypothetical institution, nearly all teaching and administration use one language and few resources support other languages. | The example concerns institutional dominance within that setting, not inherent linguistic superiority or a claim about all Pakistan.
Minority language | A community has active home-language conversations but limited materials in the local school. | Minority status here concerns support and power in context, not reduced expressive capacity.
First language | A child first develops Saraiki, later studies Urdu, and eventually uses Urdu more at work. | Earliest acquisition remains historical even if current dominance changes.
Language domain | Zain uses English for laboratory reports, Urdu with university staff and Punjabi with grandparents. | Domains connect language use with recurrent activities, partners and purposes.
Biliteracy | A student reads and writes both Urdu essays and English reports but speaks Punjabi at home without reading it. | Biliteracy concerns the two written-language abilities, not every language the student speaks.
Bimodal bilingualism | A person uses spoken Urdu and Pakistan Sign Language with different partners. | Different broad modalities distinguish this case from two spoken languages.
Code-switching | In an Urdu discussion, a lecturer quotes a complete English instruction and then resumes Urdu. | Alternation across discourse illustrates a common broad use of code-switching.
Code-mixing | Using the stated insertion convention, a speaker places the English noun assignment within an Urdu sentence. | The observable pattern is within-utterance combination; authors may also call it code-switching.
Borrowing | A source-language word becomes conventional in local Urdu and is used by people who do not know that source language. | Community adoption means the word's origin alone cannot establish each current speaker's bilingual proficiency.
Translanguaging | Students use Punjabi and Urdu to discuss a concept before drafting an English explanation with explicit genre instruction. | Flexible meaning-making can support rather than eliminate a specified language-learning goal.
Language maintenance | A family makes regular opportunities for children to talk with willing Punjabi-speaking relatives about everyday events. | Meaningful continued use supports maintenance beyond symbolic display of the language name.
Language shift | Grandparents mainly use one community language, parents use two, and children increasingly use only the school language. | The changing pattern concerns family transmission across generations.
Language attrition | After years of little Punjabi use, an adult retrieves some familiar expressions more slowly while retaining useful understanding. | Reduced accessibility can be selective and does not imply complete erasure.
Metalinguistic awareness | A learner explains that kitab and book use different forms for a related concept and notices different spelling conventions. | The learner reflects on linguistic form and meaning rather than simply naming an object.
Distributed vocabulary | A child knows farming terms mainly in Punjabi and school science terms mainly in English. | One language's naming score does not capture every concept learned through the other language.
Language history | An SLP asks when regular Punjabi, Urdu and English interaction began, who uses each, and for which daily activities. | The answers describe opportunity and use more precisely than a single bilingual label.
Historical semilingualism label | An assessor replaces the label semilingual with a profile of vocabulary, grammar, processing, expression, functions and figurative meanings across the child's opportunities. | Specific abilities and evidence are more informative than assuming a generalized deficient identity.
Language difference | A recently enrolled child uses an English pattern consistent with prior Punjabi experience while communicating effectively at home. | The difference may reflect learning history; investigate before attributing it to impairment.
Language exposure | A caregiver describes Urdu story conversations, Punjabi play and English classroom interaction separately from background media. | Duration, accessibility and reciprocal interaction contribute different information about learning conditions.
Responsive interaction | During shared play, a parent responds to the child's Punjabi comment and adds a useful word without taking over the interaction. | A contingent response builds on the child's focus and offers meaningful language experience.
Home-language support | An Urdu-medium school learner continues rich Punjabi storytelling with grandparents while receiving Urdu learning opportunities. | Supporting family communication and supporting school-language development can coexist.
Concept-based teaching | An SLP sorts familiar clothes by use, then practices the Urdu and Punjabi labels and useful requests. | The concept and its language-specific forms are connected rather than treated as competing targets.
Functional vocabulary | A child learns words and expressions needed to request water, ask for help and find school materials. | Selection follows everyday communication purposes rather than the length of a generic word list.
Narrative intervention | A child recounts a familiar bus journey using appropriate characters, events and an outcome, with family feedback on a natural telling. | Narrative organization is practiced through meaningful experience without imposing one universal story style.
Caregiver coaching | An SLP demonstrates expansions during a familiar Urdu play routine, then asks the caregiver to try and discuss what fits home life. | Coaching includes shared understanding and feasible practice, not simply issuing a worksheet.
Home practice | A family chooses five minutes of conversational practice during a routine it can realistically sustain. | Time and language access are considered; the example is a negotiated routine, not a universal dosage prescription.
Cross-language generalization | After Urdu narrative work, a clinician samples an untrained English narrative at planned intervals. | Change must be measured in the untrained language before transfer can be claimed.
Dynamic assessment | An SLP samples a novel story task, teaches a strategy and observes how the child applies it to a new story. | The supported learning response adds information beyond an initial static score.
Conceptual vocabulary scoring | A child knows 28 Urdu labels and 22 English labels, with ten translation-equivalent pairs and no other overlap. | Total word forms are 50; counting each duplicated concept once gives 40 distinct concepts under these assumptions.
Appropriate normative comparison | A clinician uses a translated task descriptively and documents that its original English percentiles may not apply. | Understanding a translated instruction does not establish equivalent item difficulty or valid norms.
Functional participation goal | A family prioritizes the child's ability to ask Punjabi-speaking grandparents for help and Urdu-speaking teachers for clarification. | Meaningful outcomes are defined with the relevant partners and settings.
Family-centered decision | An SLP asks which languages are needed for caregiving and school, then agrees practical goals and available support with the family. | Neither institutional prestige nor clinician convenience alone decides the plan.
Second-language acquisition | A learner begins regular English interaction after years of Urdu use and develops different abilities across discussion and writing. | Additional-language development is shaped by experience and task rather than a single all-or-none change.
Pre-production stage | A new English learner follows a demonstrated classroom routine and points to needed materials while saying little English. | Limited overt output can coexist with comprehension and meaningful participation.
Early production stage | A learner answers a supported classroom question with a short English phrase while expressing more complex ideas in Urdu. | New-language production length is not a direct measure of total conceptual ability.
Speech emergence stage | A child explains a familiar English-medium class activity using simple sentences with developing grammar. | Increasing production and expected errors can coexist during learning.
Intermediate fluency stage | A learner contributes complex ideas in English but asks what a new academic expression means. | Complexity does not eliminate all support and clarification needs.
Advanced fluency stage | A fluent graduate begins learning the specialized language of a new discipline. | Advanced performance in familiar contexts does not end vocabulary and genre learning.
Basic interpersonal communicative skills (BICS) | A pupil chats in English about a visible playground game with friends who share the situation. | Shared context and familiar interaction can support conversational fluency without demonstrating every academic demand.
Cognitive academic language proficiency (CALP) | The same pupil must explain contrasting research findings in an English paragraph using explicit evidence and relationships. | The task requires academic language resources and organization not fully established by playground conversation.
Common underlying proficiency (CUP) | A learner understands cause and effect in Urdu, then learns English expressions for explaining the same relationship. | Some knowledge can support both languages while the new surface forms still need learning.
Context-demand matrix | Learners compare two predictions while manipulating a visible science model. | The task can be context-embedded and cognitively demanding at the same time.
Scaffolding | A teacher models a causal explanation, prompts guided attempts and reduces help as the learner gains control. | Support is adjusted toward independence rather than removed immediately or retained unchanged forever.
Academic vocabulary | Students practice because, however and evidence while explaining a real classroom comparison. | The words are connected with academic relationships and purposes, not taught only as isolated spellings.
Prior knowledge | A child explains crop irrigation through Punjabi examples but needs English equipment labels for a school report. | Existing conceptual understanding should be separated from access to the required English expressions.
Culture | Two Urdu-speaking families describe different routines for involving grandparents in a consultation. | Shared language does not establish identical family practices or preferences.
Within-group variation | Two Lahore classmates with similar affiliations prefer different forms of address in formal conversation. | An apparent group membership does not fully predict individual interactional choices.
Language and culture | Urdu terms such as aap and tum can convey relationship and formality, with interpretation depending on the speakers and context. | Social meaning is learned through use; one word is not universally appropriate merely because speakers share nationality.
Arbitrariness of signs | Urdu kitab and English book use different conventional forms for related referents. | Arbitrariness concerns the form-meaning connection, not absence of rules or shared meaning.
Productivity | A pupil describes a newly designed bus shelter with a sentence the teacher has never modeled. | Existing linguistic resources support a novel message through patterned combination.
Gender and language | A researcher compares the same task across several groups and examines variation within groups before discussing a local pattern. | A context-sensitive study avoids treating a group average as a universal natural law.
Negative concord | A linguist analyzes a negative-concord English utterance according to the community variety before comparing formal school-writing conventions. | Community grammar and institutional editing rules are different reference systems.
Euphemism | In a sensitive Urdu conversation, a speaker uses inteqal kar gaye as a less direct expression for a person's death. | Whether wording is considerate depends on audience and context; the example illustrates intention, not a rule for every family.
Eye-contact interpretation | A Lahore pupil looks down during a formal exchange but accurately restates the teacher's instruction. | The observed understanding shows why gaze alone cannot establish comprehension or disorder.
Proxemics | A clinician asks a family whether a different seating distance would make conversation more comfortable. | Spatial preferences should be checked in the encounter rather than assigned from nationality.
Cultural humility | An SLP says that a general community description may not fit this family and invites correction. | Acknowledging limits supports individual inquiry rather than requiring the family to confirm a stereotype.
Dialect | Two varieties differ in vowel patterns, some vocabulary and grammatical choices while both remain systematic. | Dialect is broader than accent and does not mean a broken version of a standard.
Accent | Two students use the same sentence structure and words but pronounce some vowels differently. | The specified contrast is pronunciation; no grammar difference or diagnosis has been demonstrated.
Idiolect | Two siblings share a regional variety, but one repeatedly prefers certain expressions and intonation patterns. | Individual habits exist within shared community patterns.
Standard dialect | A school specifies formal Urdu writing conventions while pupils also use community varieties in conversation. | Institutional norms serve a particular function and do not demonstrate that other varieties lack grammar.
Covert prestige | Friends value a local Punjabi feature as a sign of belonging even when a formal institution discourages it. | Local positive value can coexist with wider stigma.
Register | An SLP uses assessment terminology with colleagues and explains the same idea in accessible Urdu to a family. | Purpose and audience influence situational language choices.
Style shifting | A student uses familiar local forms with friends and adjusts forms during a formal university presentation. | The shift expands contextual choices without proving that the earlier variety is defective.
Dialect-informed assessment | A clinician checks whether a child's grammatical pattern is expected in the reported variety before counting it as an impairment marker. | Appropriate background interpretation prevents confusing social difference with a clinical difficulty.
Hindko | A family reports Hindko, and an SLP records that identification while asking about actual sounds, structures and use. | A broad slide heading should not override community identification or substitute for a linguistic profile.
Doabi | A family with Doaba roots maintains some Doabi features after migration to another city. | Historical regional association is compatible with use outside the original area.
Vitality | A community has written materials, but few children are currently learning to use the language in interaction. | Codification and documentation do not independently guarantee active transmission.
De facto norms | Speakers share expectations for a greeting even though no official style guide describes them. | Norms can operate through practice without formal codification.
''')
